---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---

<Docs
	page="configuration"
	title="Configuration"
	lede="Every option, where it comes from, and which layer wins."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Where settings come from</h2>
		<p>
			squill reads the nearest <code>squill.toml</code> (or{" "}
			<code>.config/squill.toml</code>) at or above each file it formats, and
			flags override it. The defaults are the house style; everything is
			optional, and a project with no config file is a supported setup.
		</p>
		<p>
			The config can be YAML instead, as <code>squill.yaml</code> or{" "}
			<code>squill.yml</code> &mdash; <a href="docs/configuration/#yaml">the
			same keys and rules</a>. Two configs for one directory is an error, not
			a guess.
		</p>
		<p>
			<code>squill init</code> writes a starter config. It lists the languages
			squill has grammars for as checkboxes &mdash; pre-checking the ones
			where it already finds SQL &mdash; then asks for the dialect of plain{" "}
			<code>.sql</code> files and of each language you chose.{" "}
			<code>--dialect</code> sets where those answers start, and{" "}
			<code>--yes</code> (or running without a terminal) takes every
			default. <code>--yaml</code> writes <code>squill.yaml</code>.
		</p>
		<p>
			The search upward stops at a git repository root, a mount point, or a
			symlinked directory. A stray config in a scratch directory or a home
			directory therefore never reaches inside a checkout &mdash; if a config
			governs your file, it lives in your project.
		</p>
		<div class="note">
			<p>
				Layers, lowest first: built-in defaults, the config file&rsquo;s
				top-level keys, every <a href="docs/configuration/#rules">rule</a>{" "}
				that matches the file being formatted (in file order), then
				command-line flags.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Options</h2>
		<p>
			Every key has a matching flag, and flags win over both config layers.
			The flags with no key &mdash; <code>--check</code>,{" "}
			<code>--strict</code>, <code>--locked</code>, and the rest &mdash; are
			on the <a href="docs/cli/">command line</a> page.
		</p>

		<h3>Style</h3>
		<p>How the output looks. All of these can differ from rule to rule.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
			<table class="options">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>key</th><th>flag</th><th>values</th><th>default</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>indent</code></td>
						<td><code>--indent</code></td>
						<td><code>tabs</code> | <code>spaces</code></td>
						<td>
							<code>tabs</code>; embedded SQL, the <a
								href="docs/embedded/#indentation">host file&rsquo;s</a
							>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>indent-width</code></td>
						<td><code>--indent-width</code></td>
						<td>width of one level, and the measure of a tab; 1 to 16</td>
						<td>
							<code>2</code>; embedded SQL, the <a
								href="docs/embedded/#indentation">host file&rsquo;s</a
							>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>max-width</code></td>
						<td><code>--max-width</code></td>
						<td>target line width, 20 to 500</td>
						<td><code>80</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>keyword-case</code></td>
						<td><code>--keyword-case</code></td>
						<td><code>lower</code> | <code>upper</code></td>
						<td><code>lower</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>quote-idents</code></td>
						<td><code>--quote-idents</code></td>
						<td><code>as-needed</code> | <code>always</code></td>
						<td><code>as-needed</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>trailing-semicolons</code></td>
						<td><code>--trailing-semicolons</code></td>
						<td
							><code>always</code> | <code>none</code>: whether the last
							statement ends in <code>;</code></td
						>
						<td
							><code>always</code> (<code>none</code> for embedded SQL)</td
						>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Dialect and parameters</h3>
		<p>
			How squill reads the SQL. The <code>*-params</code> keys make a
			driver&rsquo;s placeholders lex as parameters where the dialect has
			other plans for them &mdash; in Postgres, <code>?</code> and{" "}
			<code>@</code> are operators and <code>%</code> is modulo. They&rsquo;re
			all off until you turn them on: which one you want depends on the
			driver, not the language. Each recipe says which its library needs.
		</p>
		<div class="scroll">
			<table class="options">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>key</th><th>flag</th><th>values</th><th>default</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>dialect</code></td>
						<td><code>--dialect</code></td>
						<td
							><code>postgres</code> | <code>sqlite</code>; in embedded SQL,
							a <a href="docs/embedded/#dialects">query can set</a> a
							string&rsquo;s own</td
						>
						<td><code>postgres</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>at-params</code></td>
						<td><code>--at-params</code></td>
						<td
							>lex <a
								href="https://docs.sqlc.dev/en/latest/howto/named_parameters.html"
								>sqlc-style</a
							> and ADO.NET-style <code>@name</code> parameters, and
							Dapper&rsquo;s <code>in @ids</code></td
						>
						<td><code>false</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>question-params</code></td>
						<td><code>--question-params</code></td>
						<td>lex JDBC-style <code>?</code> parameters in Postgres</td>
						<td><code>false</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>pyformat-params</code></td>
						<td><code>--pyformat-params</code></td>
						<td
							>lex Python DB-API <code>%s</code> / <code>%(name)s</code>
							parameters</td
						>
						<td><code>false</code></td>
					</tr>

				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Which files</h3>
		<p>
			What squill leaves alone. These apply to the whole config file, so
			they belong at its top level rather than in a rule; see{" "}
			<a href="docs/configuration/#skipping-files">skipping files</a> and{" "}
			<a href="docs/frozen/">frozen paths</a>.
		</p>
		<div class="scroll">
			<table class="options">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>key</th><th>flag</th><th>values</th><th>default</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><code>ignore</code></td>
						<td><code>--ignore</code></td>
						<td>glob patterns to skip when recursing</td>
						<td><code>[]</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>frozen</code></td>
						<td><code>--frozen</code></td>
						<td
							>glob patterns that are immutable once on the baseline ref (see{" "}
							<a href="docs/frozen/">frozen paths</a>)</td
						>
						<td><code>[]</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>frozen-ref</code></td>
						<td><code>--frozen-ref</code></td>
						<td>the baseline ref <code>frozen</code> compares against</td>
						<td>discovered from the remote</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>frozen-fetch</code></td>
						<td><code>--no-frozen-fetch</code></td>
						<td>let <code>frozen</code> ask the remote for its HEAD when it is
							not recorded locally</td>
						<td><code>true</code></td>
					</tr>

				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="skipping-files">
		<h2>Skipping files</h2>
		<p>
			When recursing directories squill honors <code>.gitignore</code> and skips
			hidden files. <code>ignore</code> skips more, with <code>*</code>,{" "}
			<code>**</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>[abc]</code>, and{" "}
			<code>&lbrace;a,b&rbrace;</code> glob syntax. Patterns are relative to the
			config file that declares them.
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`ignore = ["legacy/**", "*.gen.sql"]`} />
		<p>
			A file named explicitly on the command line always formats, ignore
			patterns included &mdash; if you asked for that file by name, you meant
			it. <a href="docs/frozen/">Frozen paths</a> are the deliberate exception.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="rules">
		<h2>Rules</h2>
		<p>
			Rules scope settings to paths. There are two kinds, each written as a
			TOML array of tables, and each needs an <code>include</code> list of
			glob patterns (relative to the config, like <code>ignore</code>). Every
			rule whose <code>include</code> matches a file applies, in file order,
			later rules winning key by key.
		</p>
		<h3>[[files]]</h3>
		<p>
			Plain SQL files. <code>*.sql</code> files are always formatted; a{" "}
			<code>[[files]]</code> rule sets options for the ones it matches, and
			brings in files by other names &mdash; a mixed-dialect tree needs no
			second config:
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`dialect = "postgres"

[[files]]
include = ["**/*.sql.sqlite", "storage/sqlite/**"]
dialect = "sqlite"

[[files]]
include = ["**/*.sql.postgres"]`} />
		<h3>[[embedded]]</h3>
		<p>
			SQL inside host-language files. A rule names the tree-sitter{" "}
			<code>grammar</code> that parses the file and, optionally, the{" "}
			<code>query</code> that finds SQL in it; matching files are formatted
			whenever squill walks past them. See{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/">embedded SQL</a> for the grammars and queries.
		</p>
		<Code lang="toml" file="squill.toml" code={`indent = "tabs"

[[embedded]]
include = ["**/*.rs"]
grammar = "rust"
dialect = "sqlite"

[[embedded]]
include = ["web/**/*.ts", "web/**/*.tsx"]
grammar = "typescript"
indent = "spaces"
indent-width = 2`} />
		<p>
			A rule without a <code>grammar</code> only layers options &mdash; say, a
			different dialect for one directory of a language another rule already
			covers. A file some <code>[[embedded]]</code> rule gives a grammar is a
			host file, and only <code>[[embedded]]</code> rules apply to it;
			otherwise only <code>[[files]]</code> rules do.
		</p>
		<p>
			The path-selecting keys &mdash; <code>ignore</code>, <code>frozen</code>,{" "}
			<code>frozen-ref</code>, and <code>frozen-fetch</code> &mdash; apply to
			the whole config file, so they stay at the top level. A misplaced key, an
			unknown grammar, or a rule without <code>include</code> is an error with
			a line number rather than a silent surprise.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="yaml">
		<h2>YAML</h2>
		<p>
			In <code>squill.yaml</code>, the top-level keys are a mapping and each
			kind of rule is a list under its name. This is the rules example above:
		</p>
		<Code lang="yaml" file="squill.yaml" code={`dialect: postgres
indent: tabs

files:
  - include: ["**/*.sql.sqlite", "storage/sqlite/**"]
    dialect: sqlite

embedded:
  - include: ["**/*.rs"]
    grammar: rust
    dialect: sqlite
  - include: ["web/**/*.ts", "web/**/*.tsx"]
    grammar: typescript
    indent: spaces
    indent-width: 2`} />
		<p>
			A file holds one YAML document. Grammars downloaded from https URLs are
			locked in the same <code>squill.lock</code> either way.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
